  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1155 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThe aforementioned northwest flow aloft regime will likely be most \nfavorable for our area Friday evening and as the high shifts further \nwest into the CA/NV/AZ/UT area on Saturday, we should see the flow \nbecome more northerly (and subsequently less favorable for precip). \nTo our northeast during this time, a pronounced trough will drop out \nof the northern plains into the Ohio Valley.  Into the start of next \nweek, as troughing remains across the eastern CONUS, the western \nhigh should start to ease back this way although under a flatter \nmorphology as systems become more notably strong along the US/CAN \nborder.\n\nStorm chances look much better Friday evening through the overnight \nhours as northwest flow combines with a southward moving frontal \nboundary.  That said, there are concerns in terms of CAPE as rather \nwarm temps are indicated at and above H5.  Forecast soundings, \ndespite the hot surface temps and near-dry-adiabatic lapse rates in \nthe lower half, show stark limited availability for meaningful deep \nconvection. There may be 3-4 thousand feet of upward energy before \nthe warmer environmental temperatures aloft taper updrafts. Guidance \ngenerally depicts MUCAPE under 800 J/kg though shear of 20-30 knots \nwill certainly aid the situation if the mid-level temperatures are \ncooler than forecast.  Some lingering storms remain possible into \nSaturday.  The HRRR is fairly bullish on coverage and intensity, but \nwhere all that moisture will come from (and energy) remains \nuncertain.\n\nBehind the front, cooler theta-e air (not necessarily dry-bulb \ncooler) will make its way into the CWFA from the northeast.  Not to \nworry though, with the high beginning to move back this way Sunday - \nnext Thursday, the warm temperatures aren't going anywhere. However, \nSunday and Monday may feel a bit nicer in the evening with dewpoints \nperhaps dropping into the 40s.",
